                <pb facs="0700" n="442" sameAs="frus:frus1915Supp/pg442"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72/1885</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Germany</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                    >Gerard</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Berlin</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1915-06-18T15:00:00+01:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">June 18, 1915, 3 p. m.</hi>
                        </date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received June 19, 4.20 p. m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>2456. Can learn nothing definite as to Germany’s answer to note. Probably nothing
                    yet decided. Army and navy against any concession. Only solutions talked of are
                    either plan for certified passenger ships already outlined to you, or mediation
                    by our President of question of submarine warfare and blockade of Germany.
                    Situation complicated by personal feuds between Von Tirpitz, Von Müller, Von Jagow, Chancellor and Falkenhayn. Von Tirpitz being against Chancellor and Von Jagow; Falkenhayn inclined to approve; Von Tirpitz neutral.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gerard</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
